• Naming Rules & Conventions (PEP 8): Variables and functions use snake_case (e.g., fat_steps, 
rate_per_litre). Constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., BASE_RATE). Names must start with a letter or 
underscore, contain only alphanumeric characters/underscores, and avoid Python keywords. 
• No True Constants in Python: Python does not enforce immutability with a const keyword; UPPER_CASE is 
a strict convention telling developers not to rebind the name. 
• Dynamic Typing: Variables are labels bound to objects, not typed memory boxes. Assigning x = 30 (int) and 
later x = '30' (str) simply rebinds x to a new object, which can cause TypeError if arithmetic is later 
attempted on x. 
• Multiple Assignment & Swapping: Python supports tuple unpacking: fat, snf = 3.5, 8.5 and swapping 
without a temporary variable: a, b = b, a. 
Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis 
Magic numbers (typing 30.0, 0.40, 0.30 inline across k expressions) require updating k locations during a rate 
revision—causing partial-update bugs. Storing rates in a dictionary RATE_CARD = {'base': 30.0, 'fat': 0.40, 'snf': 
0.30} or named constants requires only 1 edit. Both constants and dictionary lookups run in O(1) time; named 
constants use direct global/local variable lookup (LOAD_GLOBAL) with negligible memory overhead compared 
to a dict object (~232 bytes). 
