• Precedence & Associativity: Parentheses () evaluate first, followed by exponentiation ** (right-to-left, e.g., 
2 ** 3 ** 2 = 512), unary minus -x (note: -3 ** 2 evaluates to -(3**2) = -9), multiplication/division *, /, //, % 
(left-to-right), and addition/subtraction +, - (left-to-right). 
• Fixing Step Precision: (4.2 - 3.5) / 0.1 yields 7.000000000000001 due to binary subtraction inaccuracy. 
Wrapping in round(...) produces the exact integer 7. 
• round() vs int() vs math.floor() on Negatives: For -3.7: round(-3.7) -> -4 (nearest integer; ties round to 
even: round(-2.5) -> -2); int(-3.7) -> -3 (truncates toward zero); math.floor(-3.7) -> -4 (rounds toward 
negative infinity). 
• Comparison Chaining & Booleans: 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0 is equivalent to (2.0 <= fat) and (fat <= 7.0), evaluating 
fat once with short-circuit logic. 
Three Surprising Expressions Explained 
• 1. (4.2 - 3.5) / 0.1 -> 7.000000000000001: 4.2 and 3.5 cannot be represented exactly in binary float, 
leaving a tiny positive residual when divided by 0.1. 
• 2. round(2.675, 2) -> 2.67: 2.675 is actually stored as 2.67499999999999982236... in IEEE-754 float, which 
is slightly closer to 2.67 than 2.68. 
• 3. -3 ** 2 -> -9: Exponentiation ** has higher precedence than unary minus -, so Python parses it as -(3 ** 
2) rather than (-3) ** 2. 
Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis 
Using int() (truncation) or math.floor() instead of round() breaks step counts due to binary float inaccuracy: 
• For fat = 4.2: (4.2 - 3.5) / 0.1 = 7.000000000000001 -> round gives 7 (Rs 32.80), int gives 7 (Rs 32.80), floor 
gives 7 (Rs 32.80). 
• For fat = 3.1: (3.1 - 3.5) / 0.1 = -3.999999999999999 -> round gives -4 (Rs 28.40), int truncates toward zero 
giving -3 (Rs 28.80 — overpaying by Rs 0.40!), floor gives -4 (Rs 28.40). All three operations execute in O(1) 
time and O(1) space. 
