TASK 6: Organise the Logic into Functions (K3 – Apply | 4 Marks) 
BASE_RATE = 30.00 
FAT_STEP_VALUE = 0.40 
SNF_STEP_VALUE = 0.30 
 
def rate_per_litre(fat: float, snf: float) -> float: 
    """Compute rate per litre (Rs) from fat % and SNF %.""" 
    fat_steps = round((fat - 3.5) / 0.1) 
    snf_steps = round((snf - 8.5) / 0.1) 
    rate = BASE_RATE + (fat_steps * FAT_STEP_VALUE) + (snf_steps * SNF_STEP_VALUE) 
    return round(rate, 2) 
 
def payout(litres: float, fat: float, snf: float) -> float: 
    """Compute total payout (Rs) for a single milk delivery.""" 
    return round(litres * rate_per_litre(fat, snf), 2) 
 
def ten_day_total(entries: list) -> float: 
    """Sum payouts across all valid entries in a 10-day billing period.""" 
    total = 0.0 
    for litres, fat, snf in entries: 
        total = round(total + payout(litres, fat, snf), 2) 
    return total 
 
• Return vs Print: return sends the computed value back to the caller so it can be summed, tested with 
assert, or saved to a file. print() only displays text on screen and returns None. 
• Local vs Global Scope: Parameters (fat, snf) and variables created inside a function are local. Module 
constants (BASE_RATE) reside in the global scope and can be read freely inside functions, but must never 
be mutated inside functions to prevent side effects. 
Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis 
Writing one monolithic script without functions duplicates the rate formula everywhere a calculation is 
needed. Using lambda expressions (e.g., rate = lambda f, s: ...) restricts logic to a single expression and omits 
docstrings and clear debugging tracebacks. Function call overhead in Python is ~50–80 nanoseconds per call; 
for n = 150,000 entries, this adds ~0.01 seconds total, keeping time complexity O(n) while vastly improving 
maintainability. 
