steps) and SNF from 6.0 to 10.0 (41 steps), there are at most k = 51 × 41 = 2,091 unique (fat, snf) pairs. 
Precomputing or caching rates in a dictionary reduces per-entry rate lookup to O(1) hash lookup (~0.05 s 
for 150,000 entries vs ~0.19 s recalculating), using negligible extra space O(k) (~150 KB for 2,091 entries). 
TASK 11: Recommend and Defend the Final Design (K5 – Evaluate | 4 Marks) 
• Recommended Types & Rounding Rule: Use Decimal initialized from strings for all monetary values (rates, 
payouts, totals) with ROUND_HALF_UP to 2 decimal places per payment. Our Task 2 and Task 10 tests 
proved float accumulates drift across 150,000 records, while Decimal executes 150,000 records in under 1 
second with zero paisa discrepancy. 
• Carrying Out a Rate Revision: All base rates and step values are isolated as named constants in 
milkrate.py. Updating BASE_RATE or step values in that single module updates every downstream billing 
script immediately. 
• Limitations & District-Union Scalability: At 150,000 farmers (3,000,000 entries per 10-day cycle), Python 
runtime remains fast (~18 seconds total for 3M entries), though single-file CSV storage would become I/O
bound and should transition to a relational database (SQLite/PostgreSQL) storing amounts in integer paise. 
• Alternative Concept (External JSON/Config File): Reading rates from rate_card.json at startup adds ~0.001 
seconds O(1) startup I/O and ~1 KB memory, but allows co-operative administrators to update seasonal 
rates without modifying or redeploying Python source code—eliminating code-edit risks. 
